WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.480
Hi everyone, my name is Jenny Lanier&nbsp;
and I'm with the IBM Cloud team.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:10.240
Let's talk about how to deploy and manage&nbsp;
applications in IBM Cloud Code Engine.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:14.560
Before we begin, let's first talk&nbsp;
about what an "application" is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:20.000
An application is any piece of code that is&nbsp;
typically meant to run and respond to incoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:25.360
requests - such as a web server. While it is&nbsp;
also possible to have an application running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:29.840
even without incoming requests, we're&nbsp;
going to focus on the more common case.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.240
With that definition, let's jump&nbsp;
right into deploying an application.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:40.400
From this Code Engine "overview" page you'll see&nbsp;
that you can immediately deploy a container image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:43.840
that you've already built by&nbsp;
using this "quick start" dialog.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:48.560
You'll see that the image name is pre-populated&nbsp;
with a sample container image name, so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:51.680
let's just go ahead and hit "Start creating".

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:58.960
On this page you'll see there are some&nbsp;
options available, but let's keep this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:03.760
really simple, just using the defaults for&nbsp;
everything, including the name of the application.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.280
So, since we're not going to change anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:11.840
let's just go ahead and hit the&nbsp;
"Create" button to actually do it.

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:17.360
Deploying the app will take a second or two, so&nbsp;
let's discuss what's going on behind the scenes.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.800
First, Code Engine will download the image&nbsp;
you provided and then deploy that to the Code&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:28.080
Engine runtime. Since applications are meant&nbsp;
to accept requests, it will also setup all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.160
of the networking infrastructure necessary&nbsp;
to route the requests to your application.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:37.680
Let's go to the "Runtime" tab to see what kind of&nbsp;
runtime configuration options are available to us.

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:41.600
Notice on here you can control&nbsp;
things like the amount of memory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:44.480
and CPU allocated for each instance of your app.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:49.120
You can also control incoming&nbsp;
requests and automatically terminate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:51.040
any request after a certain amount of time.

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:56.080
The "Concurrency" value controls how many&nbsp;
requests your app can handle at one time and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:01.200
once that limit is reached, Code Engine will&nbsp;
create additional instances to handle the load.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:05.280
Likewise, as the load decreases it will shut down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:09.760
unneeded instances to automatically manage&nbsp;
the scaling of your application for you.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:17.040
Finally, notice the final two config options&nbsp;
- min scale and max scale. This allows you to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:21.520
control that scaling we just talked about even&nbsp;
down to zero instances if there are no incoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:26.240
requests - but you are in full control of the&nbsp;
lower and upper bounds of this scaling.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:30.240
Including having a certain number of&nbsp;
instances always running, if needed.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:36.720
Notice the app is now "ready" so let's go ahead&nbsp;
and see what it looks like by pressing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:38.240
"Application URL" button.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:46.800
Notice the URL starts with "https", so the&nbsp;
application is automatically secured with TLS.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:51.520
Now, let's pause here for a moment.&nbsp;
Think about what we just did.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:55.600
We deployed a secure internet-facing&nbsp;
application, that will automatically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:01.520
scale up and down with traffic - even to zero -&nbsp;
so you don`t have to pay when your app is idle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:05.120
And we did all of this with simply the&nbsp;
name of our container image.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:10.640
If you're familiar with platforms like Kubernetes,&nbsp;
think about how long it would have taken you to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:16.880
set up all of the infrastructure needed to do&nbsp;
that? The pods, deployments, replicateSets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:21.760
load-balancers, ingress... that's a lot&nbsp;
of work reduced down to one trivial step.

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:26.800
And that's the simplified developer&nbsp;
experience that Code Engine offers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:29.280
and to be honest, that developers should expect.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:34.080
That's all the time we have for today, but&nbsp;
hopefully you found this quick walk-through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:38.880
of how to manage applications in Code&nbsp;
Engine exciting and I encourage you to look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:43.071
at our other videos to see more key Code Engine&nbsp;
features. And, of course, we hope that you'll go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.071 --> 00:03:45.705
to www.ibm.com/cloud/code-engine to&nbsp;
play with it yourself.

00:03:45.705 --> 00:03:46.165
Thank you.

